.Dd July 31, 2026
.Dt LIBNOTIFY 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm notify_client_open ,
.Nm notify_subscribe ,
.Nm notify_unsubscribe ,
.Nm notify_publish ,
.Nm notify_state_set ,
.Nm notify_state_get ,
.Nm notify_next ,
.Nm notify_timer_add ,
.Nm notify_timer_cancel ,
.Nm notify_stats ,
.Nm notify_list_subscriptions ,
.Nm notify_list_timers ,
.Nm notify_client_close
.Nd bounded capability-component notifications
.Sh LIBRARY
.Li libnotify
.Pq Fl lnotify
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In notify.h
.Ft int
.Fn notify_client_open "struct notify_client **client"
.Ft int
.Fn notify_subscribe "struct notify_client *client" "const char *topic"
.Ft int
.Fn notify_unsubscribe "struct notify_client *client" "const char *topic"
.Ft int
.Fn notify_publish "struct notify_client *client" "const char *topic" "const void *payload" "size_t length"
.Ft int
.Fn notify_state_set "struct notify_client *client" "const char *topic" "uint64_t state"
.Ft int
.Fn notify_state_get "struct notify_client *client" "const char *topic" "struct notify_state_reply *state"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn notify_next "struct notify_client *client" "struct notify_event *event" "size_t capacity" "uint32_t timeout_ms"
.Ft int
.Fn notify_timer_add "struct notify_client *client" "uint64_t timer_id" "uint32_t interval_ms" "uint32_t flags"
.Ft int
.Fn notify_timer_cancel "struct notify_client *client" "uint64_t timer_id"
.Ft int
.Fn notify_stats "struct notify_client *client" "struct notify_stats *stats"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn notify_list_subscriptions "struct notify_client *client" "struct notify_subscription_info *out" "size_t capacity"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn notify_list_timers "struct notify_client *client" "struct notify_timer_info *out" "size_t capacity"
.Ft void
.Fn notify_client_close "struct notify_client *client"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library connects to the global
.Dq system.Notify
service through
.Xr libservice 3 .
It provides exact-topic publish and subscribe, bounded payloads and queues,
and owner-scoped one-shot or periodic monotonic timers.
Applications do not supply component or provider names.
.Pp
Timer identifiers are nonzero and scoped to one client session.
Intervals range from one millisecond through
.Dv NOTIFY_MAX_TIMER_INTERVAL_MS .
.Pp
.Fn notify_next
dequeues one event.
A timeout of zero polls, while
.Dv NOTIFY_TIMEOUT_INFINITE
waits without a deadline.
Finite waits use saturating deadline arithmetic, so values near
.Dv UINT32_MAX
cannot wrap into an unexpectedly short client deadline.
Each open creates an independent provider session, receive queue, request
sequence, and exchange lock.
Threads sharing one client are serialized so replies cannot cross, while a
blocking
.Fn notify_next
on one client does not stall another client.
An inherited client is invalid after
.Xr fork 2 ;
the child must open a fresh session.
.Pp
Ephemeral publications may contain up to
.Dv NOTIFY_MAX_PAYLOAD
opaque bytes whose schema belongs to the topic-specific typed library.
They must not contain secrets or the only copy of application state.
.Fn notify_state_set
stores a 64-bit topic state and atomically advances a broker-assigned
generation.
.Fn notify_state_get
returns the current router epoch, generation, and state.
Subscribers should subscribe before reading state and query the owning typed
service when a notification only identifies what changed.
.Pp
.Fn notify_list_subscriptions
and
.Fn notify_list_timers
enumerate the caller's own active exact-topic subscriptions and timers into
.Fa out ,
which has room for
.Fa capacity
entries.
Each is scoped by the router to the requesting session, so a caller can only
observe its own state, never another session's.
The library walks every provider page internally and returns the total number
the session holds.
That total may exceed
.Fa capacity :
a return value greater than
.Fa capacity
signals truncation, and only the first
.Fa capacity
entries were written.
A capacity of zero is permitted with a
.Dv NULL
.Fa out
to count without copying.
.Fn notify_list_subscriptions
fills each
.Vt struct notify_subscription_info
with the NUL-terminated
.Va topic .
.Fn notify_list_timers
fills each
.Vt struct notify_timer_info
with the
.Va timer_id ,
.Va interval_ms ,
the
.Dv NOTIFY_TIMER_F_*
.Va flags
(periodic versus one-shot), and a best-effort
.Va next_fire_ms
estimate of the milliseconds until the next expiry.
Both fail closed on any protocol or transport error, returning \-1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Pp
Every event identifies the random router epoch.
Queue overflow produces
.Dv NOTIFY_EVENT_GAP
with an exact saturated loss count.
If the provider connection dies,
.Fn notify_next
reconnects, restores exact-topic subscriptions, and returns one
.Dv NOTIFY_EVENT_RESET
for the new epoch rather than claiming continuous delivery.
If any other operation observes peer death, that operation returns the
transport error because its acceptance is unknown and is never replayed.
The dead session is retired; the next operation opens a fresh session and
restores subscriptions before sending its own request.
.Pp
Topics are dot-separated identifiers.
Empty segments, path syntax, control characters, and embedded NUL bytes are
rejected.
Published payloads are opaque bytes and publisher identity is supplied by the
trusted router.
Each session is deny-by-default until the provider has an authenticated,
identity-bound exact-topic publish and subscribe policy and a separate timer
grant.
The trusted publisher field remains part of application-level authorization;
topic permission alone does not authenticate message contents.
.Pp
A successful
.Fn notify_publish
reply means that the router evaluated the publication and attempted fanout.
It is not a durable-delivery acknowledgement.
Subscriber queues are volatile and bounded; a full queue drops the newest
event for that subscriber and reports the loss in statistics and a gap event.
Events are not replayed after subscriber or router restart.
Callers needing durable delivery require a separate typed queue protocol with
stable event identifiers, consumer offsets, acknowledgements, and
deduplication.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr bsdnotify 8
